    "id": 3,
    "title": "Product Inventory — HashMap",
    "gist": "Manage product quantities using a HashMap.",
    "description": "Question: Store the stock of laptops, mice, and keyboards in a HashMap. Sell one mouse and retrieve its remaining quantity. Data structures: HashMap<String, Integer>, with product names as keys and quantities as values. Assumed time complexity: O(1) average for insertion, update, and lookup; individual operations may take O(n) in unfavorable worst-case conditions. Space complexity: O(n). Expected output: Mouse stock = 24. Learning goals: Hashing, key-value pairs, insertion, updating, lookup, and getOrDefault(). Visualization: the live HashMap and its entries are inspected through heap capture.",
    "code": "import java.util.LinkedHashMap;\n\nclass Inventory {\n    final LinkedHashMap<String, Integer> stock = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n    Inventory() {\n        stock.put(\"Laptop\", 10);\n        stock.put(\"Mouse\", 25);\n        stock.put(\"Keyboard\", 15);\n    }\n}\n\nclass Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Inventory inventory = new Inventory();\n\n        inventory.stock.put(\"Mouse\", inventory.stock.get(\"Mouse\") - 1);\n        point(inventory.stock.get(\"Mouse\"), \"orange\");\n\n        System.out.println(\"Mouse stock = \" + inventory.stock.getOrDefault(\"Mouse\", 0));\n    }\n\n    static void visual(Object object, String type) {}\n    static void point(Object object, String color) {}\n    static void point(Object array, int index, String color) {}\n    static void point(Object matrix, int row, int column, String color) {}\n}",
